--- body ---
## Source
/verify on PR #270 (ticket 0227), 2026-06-04 -- raised independently by
all three review angles, out of scope for that diff.

## Context
0227 gave the header-key axis a self-syncing meta-test
(TestV1HeaderKeys_FixtureCoverage iterates v1HeaderKeys), but the
command axis of the strict-write guard is a hardcoded CMDS list in
tests/test_strictwrite.sh. If a future mutating subcommand enters the
dispatch without joining CMDS, the round-trip guard silently shrinks --
the exact drift 0227 exists to prevent, on the other axis. Verified
complete today (7 ticket-writers in CMDS; rm/init/install/update are
documented exclusions).

## Fix
A mirror Go meta-test asserting the mutating subset of the dispatch
registry is contained in test_strictwrite.sh's CMDS (parse the script's
CMDS= line; classify registry commands as mutating via an explicit
in-test map with the four documented exclusions). Negative control:
doctored registry copy with an extra mutating command must trip it.

## Exit criteria
- [x] Meta-test fails when a mutating dispatch command is absent from
      CMDS (negative control proves it)
- [x] The four exclusions are asserted, not silently skipped
- [x] make check passes

## Imagine (raid 2026-06-04)
- Why now: 0227 closed the header-key axis gap; /verify on PR #270 raised
  the command axis gap independently from all three review angles. Both axes
  need the same meta-test discipline or the strict-write guard silently shrinks
  when a new mutating subcommand enters the dispatch without joining CMDS.
- Scope check: test-only change; no production code touched. The dispatch is a
  non-iterable switch in main.go, but helptext.go exposes var commands
  ([]commandEntry, package main) -- a _test.go can range it directly, exactly
  as 0227 ranges v1HeaderKeys. One residual axis stays out of scope: a command
  present in the switch but absent from the commands slice would escape this
  meta-test; that is a pre-existing, separate drift vector not targetted here.
- Simplest path: iterate commands, require every entry to be explicitly classified
  (mutating OR readonly) in an in-test map -- unclassified names are a test
  failure. Compute mutating-set minus exclusions and assert equality (==, not
  just subset) with the CMDS set parsed from test_strictwrite.sh. Totality check
  closes the tautology: a new mutating command forgotten in both CMDS and the
  in-test map becomes "unclassified" and fails loud. Parse CMDS by regex on the
  single-line quoted assignment; guard that the parsed set is non-empty so a
  broken regex cannot pass vacuously. Negative control: inject "frobnicate" into
  the mutating set, assert it is reported missing from CMDS -- mirrors 0227's
  doctored-key design exactly.
- Antipattern scan: tautological-test risk is real but closed by the totality
  check (unclassified command is a failure, not a silent default). Irreducible
  residual: a mutating command misclassified as readonly cannot be caught by
  static analysis alone -- only the shell round-trip catches it if it is in CMDS.
  Acceptable, parallel to 0227's fixtures not verifying they exercise their
  validation path. No premature abstraction: Mutating bool on commandEntry would
  conflate help metadata with mutation semantics and churn all 19 literals; the
  in-test map is the right boundary.

## Plan (raid 2026-06-04)

### Actions

1. Add `TestMutatingCommandsCMDSCoverage` to
   src/go/erg_test.go (peer to `TestDispatchRegistrySync` at line 454;
   different axis -- mutating-subset vs CMDS, not switch vs registry).
   Add `"regexp"` to the import block in that file.

2. In-test classification map (all 19 registry entries, exhaustive):
   mutating: new, close, log, label, unlabel, archive, migrate
   readonly: validate, check, list, ready, next-id, spec, integration,
             version, rm, init, install, update
   Note: rm/init/install/update are readonly FOR THIS TEST (they are the
   four documented exclusions -- they write non-.erg outputs -- classified
   readonly in the map so they do not appear in the mutating set and
   therefore do not trigger a missing-from-CMDS failure).

3. Totality loop (anti-tautology core):
   `for _, c := range commands { if _, ok := classification[c.Name]; !ok {
       t.Errorf("command %q not classified ...", c.Name) } }`
   An unclassified entry (e.g. a new subcommand) fails here, not silently.

4. Parse CMDS from tests/test_strictwrite.sh:
   - Read file at path `"../../tests/test_strictwrite.sh"` (relative to
     src/go cwd, where `go test` always runs -- confirmed by Makefile
     `cd src/go && go test ./...`).
   - Apply `(?m)^CMDS="([^"]*)"` (multiline flag required: the file has a
     comment `# CMDS list below.` at line 10 and the assignment at line
     136; without `(?m)` Go's RE2 `^` anchors to start-of-text only and
     returns no match).
   - `strings.Fields` the captured group -> set.
   - Non-empty guard: if the set is empty, `t.Fatal("parsed CMDS set is
     empty -- regex mismatch or file missing, cannot validate")`.

5. Exclusions assertion (exit criterion 2 -- asserted not silently skipped):
   for each of {rm, init, install, update}: assert the name IS present in
   the classification map with value false (readonly). Fail if any exclusion
   key is absent, e.g. a typo "instal" would otherwise silently narrow the
   mutating set without notice. These four are expected readonly; the test
   records the intent explicitly.

6. Shared helper `missingFromCMDS(classification map[string]bool,
   exclusions map[string]bool, cmdsSet map[string]bool) []string`:
   iterates classification, collects names where value==true (mutating)
   and name not in exclusions, checks each against cmdsSet, returns
   missing names. Both the real assertion and the negative control call
   this helper -- ensures the negative control exercises the real
   comparison path (mirrors 0227's `headerKeysWithoutFixture` design).

7. Equality assertion (set equality, not just subset):
   (a) real: assert `missingFromCMDS(classification, exclusions, cmdsSet)`
       returns empty -- mutating minus exclusions is contained in CMDS.
   (b) surplus: assert no name in cmdsSet is absent from the mutating set
       minus exclusions -- CMDS contains no phantom (undocumented) entry.
   Together these assert exact equality.

8. Negative control (inject "frobnicate"):
   - Copy the classification map; add `"frobnicate": true`.
   - Call `missingFromCMDS(doctored, exclusions, cmdsSet)`.
   - Assert result contains "frobnicate". If not, the helper always
     reports empty and cannot catch drift.

9. Manual "prove it can fail" smoke (for execute agent to run, not
   automated): transiently edit tests/test_strictwrite.sh to remove
   "migrate" from the CMDS= line, run
   `cd src/go && go test -run TestMutatingCommandsCMDSCoverage .`,
   confirm red with "migrate missing from CMDS", revert. This verifies
   the real file-parse-then-compare path, not only the in-memory control.

### First test

Pass: `go test -run TestMutatingCommandsCMDSCoverage ./...` on today's
tree (7 mutating == 7 in CMDS).
Fail: same test after transiently removing "migrate" from CMDS= in
tests/test_strictwrite.sh -- must report "migrate missing from CMDS".
Negative control (in-test): inject "frobnicate" into classification map,
assert reported as missing; if not reported the helper has a tautology.

### Dependencies

- src/go/helptext.go: `var commands []commandEntry` (package main,
  accessible to `_test.go` in package main) -- already present.
- tests/test_strictwrite.sh: CMDS= line at line 136, double-quoted,
  single assignment -- confirmed format `CMDS="new close log ..."`.
- No new production files; test-only change.
- erg_test.go already in package main; needs `"regexp"` import added.

### Tautology scan

Risk: classification map that classifies everything as readonly would
make the mutating set empty and the equality assertion vacuously pass
against an empty CMDS. Closed by step 3 (totality loop ensures every
command is explicitly classified) and step 7b (CMDS surplus check would
then fail because CMDS has 7 entries while the mutating set is empty).
Closed independently by the non-empty guard in step 4 (if CMDS parse
fails, test Fatal-fails rather than vacuously passing).
Irreducible residual: a mutating command misclassified as readonly in the
in-test map cannot be caught by static analysis alone; only the shell
round-trip guard (test_strictwrite.sh) catches it if CMDS is also correct.
Acceptable -- parallel to 0227's fixtures not verifying they exercise the
validation path.
